%%
%% chapter-9.ltx — The Simply-Typed Lambda Calculus
%%

The simply-typed lambda calculus (STLC) is the untyped lambda calculus equipped with a type system. Despite the simplicity of its type system---only base types and function types---the STLC is one of the most important formal systems in computer science. It enjoys the \emph{strong normalization} property (every well-typed term terminates), it is intimately connected to propositional logic via the \emph{Curry--Howard correspondence}, and it provides the foundation for the type systems of modern programming languages.

This chapter develops the STLC in detail: its types, typing rules, strong normalization theorem, connections to logic and cartesian closed categories, and extensions with products, sums, unit, and empty types.

\section{Types and Typing Rules}\label{sec:stlc-types}

\begin{definition}[Types]\label{def:stlc-types}
The set of \emph{types} in the simply-typed lambda calculus is defined by:
\begin{grammar}{Types}
\tau & \longrightarrow \iota \mid \tau \to \tau
\end{grammar}
where $\iota$ ranges over a set of \emph{base types} (e.g., $\mathrm{Bool}$, $\mathrm{Nat}$, or a set of type variables).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Typing Context]\label{def:stlc-context}
A \emph{typing context} (or \emph{type environment}) $\Gamma$ is a finite mapping from variables to types: $\Gamma = \{x_1 : \tau_1, x_2 : \tau_2, \ldots, x_n : \tau_n\}$. We write $\Gamma, x : \tau$ for the context that extends $\Gamma$ with the binding $x : \tau$ (assuming $x \notin \mathrm{dom}(\Gamma)$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Typing Judgment]\label{def:stlc-judgment}
A \emph{typing judgment} has the form $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$, read as ``in context $\Gamma$, expression $e$ has type $\tau$.''
\end{definition}

\begin{typerules}{Simply-Typed Lambda Calculus}
\typeaxiom{Var}{\Gamma, x:\tau, \Gamma' \turns x : \tau} \\[8pt]
\typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x:\tau_1 \turns e : \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns \lambda x:\tau_1.\,e : \tau_1 \to \tau_2} \\[8pt]
\typerule{App}{\Gamma \turns e_1 : \tau_2 \to \tau \quad \Gamma \turns e_2 : \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns e_1\;e_2 : \tau}
\end{typerules}

\begin{definition}[Well-Typed Terms]\label{def:stlc-well-typed}
A term $e$ is \emph{well-typed} if there exists a context $\Gamma$ and a type $\tau$ such that $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$. A closed term $e$ is well-typed if $\emptyset \turns e : \tau$ for some $\tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Typing Derivation]\label{ex:stlc-typing}
We derive $\emptyset \turns \lambda x:\iota.\,x : \iota \to \iota$:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\dfrac{}{x:\iota \turns x : \iota}\textsc{Var}}{ \emptyset \turns \lambda x:\iota.\,x : \iota \to \iota}\textsc{Abs}$
\end{center}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Typing the Identity Function Applied]\label{ex:stlc-app}
We derive $\emptyset \turns (\lambda x:\iota.\,x)\;y : \iota$ where $y : \iota$:
\begin{center}
$\dfrac{\dfrac{}{y:\iota \turns \lambda x:\iota.\,x : \iota \to \iota} \quad \dfrac{}{y:\iota \turns y : \iota}}{y:\iota \turns (\lambda x:\iota.\,x)\;y : \iota}\textsc{App}$
\end{center}
\end{example}

\section{Properties of the Type System}\label{sec:stlc-properties}

\begin{lemma}[Weakening]\label{lem:stlc-weakening}
If $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ and $x \notin \mathrm{dom}(\Gamma)$, then $\Gamma, x : \sigma \turns e : \tau$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Substitution]\label{lem:stlc-substitution}
If $\Gamma, x : \sigma \turns e : \tau$ and $\Gamma \turns e' : \sigma$, then $\Gamma \turns \subst{e}{x}{e'} : \tau$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Subject Reduction (Type Soundness)]\label{thm:stlc-subject-reduction}
If $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ and $e \stepsto e'$, then $\Gamma \turns e' : \tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the derivation of $e \stepsto e'$. The key case is beta-reduction: $(\lambda x:\sigma.\,e_1)\;e_2 \stepsto \subst{e_1}{x}{e_2}$. By the typing derivation, $\Gamma, x:\sigma \turns e_1 : \tau$ and $\Gamma \turns e_2 : \sigma$. By the substitution lemma, $\Gamma \turns \subst{e_1}{x}{e_2} : \tau$.
\end{proof}

\section{Strong Normalization}\label{sec:stlc-normalization}

\begin{theorem}[Strong Normalization]\label{thm:stlc-strong-norm}
Every well-typed term in the STLC is strongly normalizing: every reduction sequence from a well-typed term terminates.
\end{theorem}

This is one of the most important properties of the STLC. It means that the type system \emph{guarantees termination}: there are no infinite loops in well-typed programs.

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The standard proof uses a \emph{reducibility candidate} argument (Tait's method) or a \emph{logical relations} argument. We sketch the logical relations approach.

Define, for each type $\tau$, a set $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ of ``reducible'' terms of type $\tau$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item For base types $\iota$: $\mathcal{R}_\iota$ is the set of all terms of type $\iota$ that are strongly normalizing.
  \item For function types $\sigma \to \tau$: $\mathcal{R}_{\sigma \to \tau}$ is the set of terms $e$ of type $\sigma \to \tau$ such that for all $e' \in \mathcal{R}_\sigma$, $e\;e' \in \mathcal{R}_\tau$.
\end{itemize}

Prove by induction on typing derivations that if $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ and $\gamma$ maps each $x_i : \tau_i \in \Gamma$ to a term in $\mathcal{R}_{\tau_i}$, then $\subst{e}{\gamma} \in \mathcal{R}_\tau$. The key case is lambda abstraction: if $\Gamma, x:\sigma \turns e : \tau$ and $\gamma$ maps variables to reducible terms, then $\lambda x.\,\subst{e}{\gamma}$ is reducible because for any $e' \in \mathcal{R}_\sigma$, $(\lambda x.\,\subst{e}{\gamma})\;e' \stepsto \subst{e}{\gamma[x \mapsto e']}$, and $e'$ is reducible, so the result is in $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ by the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No Fixed-Point Combinator]\label{cor:no-Y}
There is no fixed-point combinator in the STLC: there is no closed term $Y : (\tau \to \tau) \to \tau$ such that $Y\;F =_\beta F\;(Y\;F)$ for all $F : \tau \to \tau$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If such a $Y$ existed, then $Y\;(\lambda x:\tau.\,x\;x)$ would be well-typed (if $\tau = \sigma \to \sigma$ for some $\sigma$), but self-application $x\;x$ is not typable in the STLC.
\end{proof}

\begin{insight}
Strong normalization means that the STLC is \emph{not} Turing-complete: it cannot express all computable functions. In particular, it cannot express general recursion. This is both a strength (all programs terminate, no infinite loops) and a weakness (cannot express arbitrary computations). To recover Turing completeness while maintaining type safety, we need more sophisticated type systems (e.g., recursive types, dependent types, or a separate fixed-point operator with a type like $(\tau \to \tau) \to \tau$).
\end{insight}

\section{Curry--Howard for Propositional Logic}\label{sec:stlc-curry-howard}

The simply-typed lambda calculus is isomorphic to intuitionistic propositional logic. This is the \emph{Curry--Howard correspondence} (also called the ``Curry--Howard isomorphism'' or ``propositions-as-types'' principle).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Curry--Howard correspondence: STLC and propositional logic}
\label{tab:curry-howard}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Logic} & \textbf{STLC} & \textbf{Programming} \\
\hline
Proposition & Type & Type \\
Proof & Term & Program \\
$\varphi \to \psi$ & $\tau_1 \to \tau_2$ & Function type \\
$\varphi \land \psi$ & $\tau_1 \times \tau_2$ & Product type (pair) \\
$\varphi \lor \psi$ & $\tau_1 + \tau_2$ & Sum type (tagged union) \\
$\top$ (truth) & $\mathbf{1}$ (unit type) & Unit value \\
$\bot$ (falsity) & $\mathbf{0}$ (empty type) & No values \\
Assumption & Variable & Parameter \\
Modus ponens & Application & Function call \\
Deduction theorem & Abstraction & Lambda expression \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{theorem}[Curry--Howard Correspondence]\label{thm:curry-howard}
A proposition $\varphi$ is provable in intuitionistic propositional logic if and only if the corresponding type $\tau_\varphi$ is inhabited in the STLC (i.e., there exists a closed term $e : \tau_\varphi$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Proofs as Programs]\label{ex:proofs-programs}
\begin{itemize}
  \item The tautology $p \to p$ corresponds to the type $\iota \to \iota$, inhabited by $\lambda x:\iota.\,x$.
  \item The tautology $p \to (q \to p)$ corresponds to $\iota_1 \to (\iota_2 \to \iota_1)$, inhabited by $\lambda x:\iota_1.\,\lambda y:\iota_2.\,x$.
  \item The tautology $(p \to (q \to r)) \to ((p \to q) \to (p \to r))$ (the S combinator) corresponds to a type inhabited by $\lambda f.\,\lambda g.\,\lambda x.\,f\;x\;(g\;x)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Non-Theorems]\label{ex:non-theorems}
The law of excluded middle $p \lor \neg p$ corresponds to the type $\iota + (\iota \to \mathbf{0})$. This type is \emph{not} inhabited in the STLC, reflecting the fact that excluded middle is not provable in intuitionistic logic.
\end{example}

\section{Church vs.\ Curry Style}\label{sec:stlc-church-curry}

\begin{definition}[Church Style]\label{def:church-style}
In \emph{Church style} (also called ``explicitly typed''), terms carry type annotations:
\[\lambda x:\tau.\,e\]
Every variable binding is annotated with its type. Type checking is a simple syntax-directed pass.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Curry Style]\label{def:curry-style}
In \emph{Curry style} (also called ``implicitly typed''), terms are untyped (as in the untyped lambda calculus), and typing is a \emph{property} that terms may or may not have. Type inference is needed to determine whether a term is typable and what its type is.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The distinction between Church and Curry style is not merely notational. In Church style, types are part of the syntax, and the same term cannot have two different types (types are unique). In Curry style, type inference may find a ``principal type''---the most general type from which all other valid types can be obtained by substitution. The Hindley--Milner type system (see \chref{hm-inference}) is a Curry-style system with principal types.
\end{insight}

\section{Extensions: Products, Sums, Unit, Empty}\label{sec:stlc-extensions}

\subsection{Product Types}

\begin{typerules}{Product Types}
\typerule{Pair}{\Gamma \turns e_1 : \tau_1 \quad \Gamma \turns e_2 : \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns (e_1, e_2) : \tau_1 \times \tau_2} \\[8pt]
\typerule{Proj$_1$}{\Gamma \turns e : \tau_1 \times \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns \pi_1(e) : \tau_1} \\[8pt]
\typerule{Proj$_2$}{\Gamma \turns e : \tau_1 \times \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns \pi_2(e) : \tau_2}
\end{typerules}

The reduction rules are:
\begin{align*}
  \pi_1(e_1, e_2) &\stepsto e_1 \\
  \pi_2(e_1, e_2) &\stepsto e_2
\end{align*}

\subsection{Sum Types}

\begin{typerules}{Sum Types}
\typerule{Inl}{\Gamma \turns e : \tau_1}{\Gamma \turns \mathrm{inl}(e) : \tau_1 + \tau_2} \\[8pt]
\typerule{Inr}{\Gamma \turns e : \tau_2}{\Gamma \turns \mathrm{inr}(e) : \tau_1 + \tau_2} \\[8pt]
\typerule{Case}{\Gamma \turns e : \tau_1 + \tau_2 \quad \Gamma, x:\tau_1 \turns e_1 : \tau \quad \Gamma, y:\tau_2 \turns e_2 : \tau}{\Gamma \turns \mathrm{case}\;e\;\mathrm{of}\;\mathrm{inl}(x) \Rightarrow e_1 \mid \mathrm{inr}(y) \Rightarrow e_2 : \tau}
\end{typerules}

The reduction rules are:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{case}\;\mathrm{inl}(v)\;\mathrm{of}\;\mathrm{inl}(x) \Rightarrow e_1 \mid \mathrm{inr}(y) \Rightarrow e_2 &\stepsto \subst{e_1}{x}{v} \\
  \mathrm{case}\;\mathrm{inr}(v)\;\mathrm{of}\;\mathrm{inl}(x) \Rightarrow e_1 \mid \mathrm{inr}(y) \Rightarrow e_2 &\stepsto \subst{e_2}{y}{v}
\end{align*}

\subsection{Unit Type}

\begin{typerules}{Unit Type}
\typeaxiom{Unit}{\Gamma \turns () : \mathbf{1}}
\end{typerules}

The unit type $\mathbf{1}$ has exactly one value: $()$. It corresponds to logical truth $\top$.

\subsection{Empty Type}

\begin{typerules}{Empty Type}
\typerule{Abort}{\Gamma \turns e : \mathbf{0}}{\Gamma \turns \mathrm{abort}(e) : \tau}
\end{typerules}

The empty type $\mathbf{0}$ has no values. The $\mathrm{abort}$ rule says that from a value of type $\mathbf{0}$, we can derive anything (ex falso quodlibet). It corresponds to logical falsity $\bot$.

\section{Connections to Cartesian Closed Categories}\label{sec:stlc-ccc}

\begin{theorem}[STLC and CCCs]\label{thm:stlc-ccc}
The simply-typed lambda calculus with products, sums, unit, and empty type is the \emph{internal language} of cartesian closed categories with coproducts and an initial object. Specifically:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Every CCC gives rise to a model of the STLC (its ``internal language'')
  \item Every model of the STLC gives rise to a CCC (its ``syntactic category'')
  \item These constructions are inverse to each other (up to equivalence)
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{STLC types and CCC structure}
\label{tab:stlc-ccc}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{STLC} & \textbf{CCC} \\
\hline
Base type $\iota$ & Object $[\![\iota]\!]$ \\
Function type $\tau_1 \to \tau_2$ & Exponential $[\![\tau_2]\!]^{[\![\tau_1]\!]}$ \\
Product type $\tau_1 \times \tau_2$ & Product $[\![\tau_1]\!] \times [\![\tau_2]\!]$ \\
Unit type $\mathbf{1}$ & Terminal object $1$ \\
Term $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ & Morphism $[\![\Gamma]\!] \to [\![\tau]\!]$ \\
Abstraction $\lambda x:\tau_1.\,e$ & Currying \\
Application $e_1\;e_2$ & Evaluation \\
Pair $(e_1, e_2)$ & Pairing $\langle -, - \rangle$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
The correspondence between the STLC and CCCs is a deep structural fact: the type system of the STLC is not an ad hoc collection of rules, but a reflection of the universal properties of categorical constructions. This insight, due to Lawvere, provides a unified understanding of types, programs, and computation.
\end{insight}

\section{Type Inference for the STLC}\label{sec:stlc-inference}

\subsection{The Inference Problem}

In Curry style, terms are untyped, and we ask: does there exist a type assignment that makes this term well-typed?

\begin{definition}[Type Inference Problem]\label{def:type-inference}
Given an untyped lambda term $e$, the \emph{type inference problem} is to find a context $\Gamma$ and a type $\tau$ such that $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$, or to determine that no such assignment exists.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Unification}\label{sec:stlc-unification}

Type inference reduces to the problem of \emph{unification}: finding a substitution that makes two types equal.

\begin{definition}[Unification Problem]\label{def:unification}
A \emph{unification problem} is a finite set of equations $\{s_1 = t_1, \ldots, s_n = t_n\}$ between types. A \emph{unifier} is a substitution $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(s_i) = \sigma(t_i)$ for all $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unification Algorithm]\label{thm:unification}
There exists an algorithm that, given a unification problem, either finds a most general unifier (mgu) or reports that no unifier exists.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Robinson's unification algorithm: repeatedly decompose equations ($s \to t = s' \to t'$ becomes $s = s'$ and $t = t'$), eliminate variables ($x = t$ becomes $\subst{}{x}{t}$ applied to remaining equations), and check for occurs-check failures ($x = t$ where $x$ occurs in $t$).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Principal Type]\label{def:principal-type}
A type $\tau$ is the \emph{principal type} of a term $e$ if $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ and for every other typing $\Gamma' \turns e : \tau'$, there exists a substitution $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(\tau) = \tau'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Principal Type Property for STLC]\label{thm:principal-type}
Every typable term in the STLC has a principal type.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the unification-based type inference algorithm. The algorithm computes the most general unifier at each step, which yields the most general (principal) type.
\end{proof}

\section{Logical Relations}\label{sec:stlc-logical-relations}

Logical relations are a powerful technique for proving properties of the STLC.

\begin{definition}[Logical Relation]\label{def:logical-relation}
A \emph{logical relation} $\mathcal{R}$ on the STLC is a family of relations $\{\mathcal{R}_\tau\}$ indexed by types, such that for function types:
\[(e_1, e_2) \in \mathcal{R}_{\tau_1 \to \tau_2} \iff \forall (v_1, v_2) \in \mathcal{R}_{\tau_1}.\,(e_1\;v_1, e_2\;v_2) \in \mathcal{R}_{\tau_2}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Contextual Equivalence]\label{ex:contextual-equiv}
Define $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ to be the identity relation on terms of type $\tau$. Then $(e_1, e_2) \in \mathcal{R}_\tau$ iff $e_1$ and $e_2$ are contextually equivalent (behave the same in all contexts).
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental Theorem of Logical Relations]\label{thm:fundamental-lr}
If $\Gamma \turns e : \tau$ and $\gamma_1, \gamma_2$ are environments that map each $x_i : \tau_i \in \Gamma$ to related values $(\gamma_1(x_i), \gamma_2(x_i)) \in \mathcal{R}_{\tau_i}$, then $(\subst{e}{\gamma_1}, \subst{e}{\gamma_2}) \in \mathcal{R}_\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the typing derivation. The key case is lambda abstraction: if $\Gamma, x:\sigma \turns e : \tau$, then for any $(v_1, v_2) \in \mathcal{R}_\sigma$, we have $(\subst{e}{\gamma_1[x \mapsto v_1]}, \subst{e}{\gamma_2[x \mapsto v_2]}) \in \mathcal{R}_\tau$ by the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\section{Parametricity}\label{sec:stlc-parametricity}

\begin{definition}[Parametric Polymorphism]\label{def:parametricity}
A polymorphic function is \emph{parametric} if it behaves uniformly for all type instantiations---it cannot inspect or depend on the specific type.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Reynolds' Abstraction Theorem]\label{thm:reynolds}
Every term of type $\forall \alpha.\,F(\alpha)$ in System F is parametrically polymorphic: it defines a natural transformation from $F$ to $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Free Theorems]\label{ex:free-theorems-stlc}
From parametricity, we can derive ``free theorems'':
\begin{itemize}
  \item A function $f : \forall \alpha.\,\alpha \to \alpha$ must be the identity.
  \item A function $f : \forall \alpha.\,\alpha \to \alpha \to \alpha$ must be either $\lambda x.\,\lambda y.\,x$ or $\lambda x.\,\lambda y.\,y$.
  \item A function $f : \forall \alpha.\,\mathrm{List}(\alpha) \to \mathrm{List}(\alpha)$ must satisfy $\mathrm{map}(g) \circ f = f \circ \mathrm{map}(g)$ for all $g$.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\section{Normalization by Evaluation}\label{sec:stlc-nbe}

\begin{definition}[Normalization by Evaluation]\label{def:nbe}
\emph{Normalization by evaluation} (NBE) is a technique for computing the normal form of a typed lambda term by:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Evaluating the term in a semantic domain (typically using HOAS)
  \item Reifying the semantic value back into a syntactic normal form
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[NBE for STLC]\label{ex:nbe-stlc}
For the STLC with base type $\iota$:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{Val}(\iota) &= \mathrm{Ne}(\iota) \quad \text{(neutral terms of base type)} \\
  \mathrm{Val}(\tau_1 \to \tau_2) &= \mathrm{Val}(\tau_1) \to \mathrm{Val}(\tau_2) \quad \text{(meta-language functions)}
\end{align*}
Evaluation maps terms to values; reification maps values back to normal forms.
\end{example}

\begin{insight}
NBE is significantly more efficient than syntactic normalization because it uses the meta-language's built-in function application instead of explicit substitution. It is the technique used in modern compilers and proof assistants for type checking and normalization.
\end{insight}

\section{Consistency and Decidability}\label{sec:stlc-consistency}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency of the STLC]\label{thm:stlc-consistency}
The empty type $\mathbf{0}$ is not inhabited in the STLC: there is no closed term $e : \mathbf{0}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By strong normalization, any closed term $e : \mathbf{0}$ would reduce to a normal form of type $\mathbf{0}$. But there are no normal forms of type $\mathbf{0}$ (no constructors). Hence no such term exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Consistency of Intuitionistic Logic]\label{cor:consistency-logic}
Intuitionistic propositional logic is consistent: $\bot$ is not provable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Decidability of Type Inference for STLC]\label{thm:stlc-decidable}
Type inference for the STLC is decidable: there exists an algorithm that, given an untyped lambda term $e$, determines whether $e$ is typable and, if so, computes its principal type.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The algorithm is based on unification (see \secref{stlc-unification}). Unification is decidable (Robinson's algorithm), and type inference reduces to a finite sequence of unification problems.
\end{proof}

\section{Semantic Interpretations of the STLC}\label{sec:stlc-semantics}

\subsection{Set-Theoretic Semantics}\label{sec:stlc-set-semantics}

The simplest semantic interpretation maps types to sets and terms to elements:
\begin{align*}
  \sembrack{\iota} &= \text{some set } D_\iota \\
  \sembrack{\tau_1 \to \tau_2} &= \sembrack{\tau_1} \to \sembrack{\tau_2} \quad \text{(set of functions)} \\
  \sembrack{\tau_1 \times \tau_2} &= \sembrack{\tau_1} \times \sembrack{\tau_2} \quad \text{(Cartesian product)}
\end{align*}

\subsection{Domain-Theoretic Semantics}\label{sec:stlc-domain-semantics}

For a more refined semantics that handles recursion and non-termination, we use domain theory:
\begin{align*}
  \sembrack{\iota} &= D_\iota \quad \text{(a pointed CPO)} \\
  \sembrack{\tau_1 \to \tau_2} &= [\sembrack{\tau_1} \to \sembrack{\tau_2}] \quad \text{(continuous functions)}
\end{align*}
The function space $[D \to E]$ of continuous functions between CPOs is itself a CPO, making this interpretation well-defined.

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Routing Type Information through a Bidirectional Checker}

In bidirectional checking, expected types flow into introductions
such as lambdas, and synthesized types flow out of variables and
applications. Consider the fragment with variables, application,
unannotated lambdas, and explicit ascriptions $(e:T)$. Inputs are
finite syntax trees, a lexical context $\Gamma$, and (in checking
mode) an expected type. Types are finite trees built from named
base types and arrows; equality is structural. Failed lookups,
failed recursive checks, and attempts to split a non-arrow as an
arrow all reject. Ascription supplies a type, but does not exempt
its expression from checking.

\begin{sudocode}[Two modes for arrow types]
\Function{Synth}{$\Gamma,e$}
  \If{$e$ is a variable $x$}
    \State \Return $\Gamma[x]$
  \ElsIf{$e=(b:T)$}
    \State $\Call{Check}{\Gamma,b,T}$ \Comment{Verify ascription}
    \State \Return $T$
  \ElsIf{$e=f\,a$}
    \State Read $\Call{Synth}{\Gamma,f}$ as $A\to B$
    \State $\Call{Check}{\Gamma,a,A}$ \Comment{Argument gets $A$}
    \State \Return $B$
  \EndIf
  \State Reject missing expected type
\EndFunction
\Procedure{Check}{$\Gamma,e,T$}
  \If{$e=\lambda x.b$}
    \State Read $T$ as $A\to B$
    \State $\Call{Check}{\Gamma[x\mapsto A],b,B}$
  \Else
    \State Require $\Call{Synth}{\Gamma,e}=T$
  \EndIf
\EndProcedure
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Let $\Gamma=\{n:\mathrm{Nat}\}$ and synthesize the type of
$((\lambda x.x):\mathrm{Nat}\to\mathrm{Nat})\,n$.
The ascription checks the lambda against the arrow and visits
its body with $x:\mathrm{Nat}$ added. Lookup synthesizes
$\mathrm{Nat}$ for $x$, matching the expected result.
The ascription now returns the arrow, so the application checks
$n$ against its domain. Lookup agrees, and the application
synthesizes $\mathrm{Nat}$. Remove the ascription and synthesis
fails at the operator lambda, even though the unannotated
expression has a type in the declarative system.

