\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Successful calls correspond to declarative typing derivations
under the supplied context. In the application case the synthesized
domain gives exactly the argument premise of the application rule.
A same-term call from checking to synthesis cannot loop: synthesis
either returns, rejects, or recurses on strict subterms. This is
a decision procedure for the specified annotation discipline, not a
complete inference procedure for the unannotated calculus.
Annotations at mode boundaries can also improve diagnostics: a
bad argument reports a mismatch against the function's domain,
rather than changing the inferred meaning of the function.

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:stlc-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Give a complete typing derivation for $\emptyset \turns \lambda f:\iota \to \iota.\,\lambda x:\iota.\,f\;(f\;x) : (\iota \to \iota) \to \iota \to \iota$.
  
  \item Prove the substitution lemma for the STLC.
  
  \item Prove subject reduction for the STLC with products and sums.
  
  \item Show that the type $(\iota \to \iota) \to \iota$ is not inhabited.
  
  \item Find a closed term of type $((\iota_1 \to \iota_2) \to \iota_1) \to \iota_1$ (Peirce's law). Is this possible?
  
  \item Prove strong normalization for the STLC with products.
  
  \item Show that the STLC with products and sums corresponds to a CCC with coproducts.
  
  \item Define a term of type $(\iota_1 \times \iota_2) \to (\iota_2 \times \iota_1)$ (swap) and verify its type.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:stlc-summary}

The simply-typed lambda calculus is a cornerstone of programming language theory:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Its type system guarantees strong normalization: all well-typed programs terminate.
  \item Via the Curry--Howard correspondence, types are propositions and programs are proofs.
  \item The STLC is the internal language of cartesian closed categories.
  \item Extensions with products, sums, unit, and empty type provide a rich type system that corresponds to intuitionistic propositional logic.
  \item The distinction between Church style (explicit types) and Curry style (type inference) is fundamental to the design of typed languages.
\end{itemize}

In the next chapter, we go beyond simple types to \emph{dependent types}, where types can depend on values, enabling the expression of arbitrary mathematical propositions as types.
